<article class="env-lemma" id="00JZ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00JZ">Lemma <span data-tag="00JZ">10.58.5</span>.</a> Suppose that $f: n \mapsto f(n) \in A$ is defined for all $n$ sufficiently large and suppose that $n \mapsto f(n) - f(n-1)$ is a numerical polynomial. Then $f$ is a numerical polynomial. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $f(n) - f(n-1) = \sum \nolimits _{i = 0}^ r \binom {n}{i} a_ i$ for all $n \gg 0$. Set $g(n) = f(n) - \sum \nolimits _{i = 0}^ r \binom {n + 1}{i + 1} a_ i$. Then $g(n) - g(n-1) = 0$ for all $n \gg 0$. Hence $g$ is eventually constant, say equal to $a_{-1}$. We leave it to the reader to show that $a_{-1} + \sum \nolimits _{i = 0}^ r \binom {n + 1}{i + 1} a_ i$ has the required shape (see remark above the lemma). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>